    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d399">

                <pb facs="0578" n="479" xml:id="pg479"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.00/2517: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-08-11T13:00:00+01:00">August 11, 1936—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 11—7:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>740. Your 304, August 10, 7 p.m. I have spoken with Bargeton<anchor n="46"
                        xml:id="d399-f1"/> who says that the report is “absolutely inexact”. He said
                    that a few days ago the French Chargé d’Affaires in Washington had been
                    instructed to inform the State Department of the proposed non-intervention pact;
                    and that while the French Government would of course be pleased if the American
                    Government should desire to adhere thereto it was not the intention of the
                    French Government to “invite” such adherence.</p>
                <p>Repeated to London.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d399-f1" n="46" xml:id="d399-f1-content">
                <p>Paul Pierre Bargeton, Director of Political and Commercial Affairs in the French
                    Foreign Office.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
